=== THE 5 AGENTS (final) ===
1. GATE HANDLING AGENT
   - Assigns flights to gates based on aircraft size, turnaround time, adjacency constraints
   - Detects/resolves gate conflicts (two flights wanting same slot)
   - Tracks gate occupancy state in real time
   - Future: taxi distance optimization, terminal congestion balancing, learned airline gate preferences

2. GROUND CREW AGENT
   - Assigns crew (baggage, pushback, cleaning) to turnarounds based on availability/shift constraints
   - Flags understaffed turnarounds, requests reprioritization
   - Tracks crew fatigue/shift-hour limits
   - Future: multi-skill crew optimization, fatigue-aware scheduling, cross-agent shared crew pool negotiation

3. ATS (SCHEDULE TRACKING) AGENT
   - Monitors scheduled vs actual arrival/departure times
   - Flags timing conflicts / cascading delay risk before critical
   - Feeds real-time schedule deltas to Orchestrator
   - Future: predictive delay modeling (ML-based ETA correction), runway/slot contention modeling

4. DISRUPTION AGENT
   - Injects/manages disruption events: weather holds, tech-unserviceable aircraft, crew shortages
   - Determines which flights/resources are affected by a disruption
   - Communicates severity/scope to Orchestrator to trigger replanning
   - Future: live weather/NOTAM data feeds, probabilistic disruption modeling, multi-event cascading disruptions

5. ORCHESTRATOR AGENT
   - Central coordinator, resolves conflicts raised by other 4 agents
   - Re-runs downstream agents' decision logic when disruption/conflict flagged
   - Maintains system-wide state consistency (single source of truth)
   - Logs every decision + rationale for dashboard reasoning trace (via Blackboard.log_decision() appending DecisionLogEntry to AirportStatus.decision_log)
   - Future: negotiation protocol (bidding/priority scoring vs hard rules), multi-objective optimization (cost vs delay vs passenger impact), replay/what-if simulation mode
   - CANDIDATE for single-writer role if Decision #19 is resolved that way

