=== core/simulator.py (ground truth) ===
Simulator class. Constructor: __init__(board: Blackboard, bus: EventBus, tick_duration_minutes: int = 5, on_tick: Callable[[Blackboard], Awaitable[None]] | None = None)
  - self.sim_time: datetime | None, starts unset, derived from earliest scheduled_arrival once load_flights() is called, no hardcoded assumption about scenario start time
  - self._pending_disruptions: list[DisruptionEvent], staged disruptions not yet due

Setup methods:
  - load_flights(flights): seeds board.flights, sets/updates sim_time to earliest scheduled_arrival
  - load_disruptions(disruptions): stages disruptions into _pending_disruptions, kept sorted by trigger_time

World clock methods (private):
  - _advance_flight_statuses(): per flight, progresses status based on sim_time vs scheduled times:
      SCHEDULED -> IN_AIR once sim_time >= scheduled_arrival (publishes "flight_departed_origin" event)
      IN_AIR -> LANDED once sim_time >= scheduled_arrival + 10min fixed taxi/landing buffer (sets actual_arrival if unset; publishes "flight_landed" event) — the 10-minute buffer is a flagged hardcoded assumption, a real ADS-B/telemetry feed would replace it
      LANDED -> AT_GATE only once flight.assigned_gate is not None (i.e. only after an agent has assigned a gate; this is the deliberate handoff point from world-clock state to agent-decision state) — KNOWN LIMITATION: with on_tick=None (no agents yet), flights stall at LANDED forever, expected until agent graph exists
      AT_GATE -> READY_FOR_PUSHBACK once sim_time >= scheduled_departure
      READY_FOR_PUSHBACK -> DEPARTED (sets actual_departure if unset, frees the gate: assigned_flight=None, gate_status=OPEN; publishes "flight_departed" event)
      CANCELLED flights are skipped entirely, frozen, no further progression
      All mutations wrapped in `async with self.board.lock`
  - _inject_due_disruptions(): moves any pending disruption with trigger_time <= sim_time into board.open_disruptions (lock-protected), publishes "disruption_triggered" event per injected disruption

Tick loop methods:
  - run_tick(): raises RuntimeError if sim_time is None (load_flights() not called yet). Sequence: _inject_due_disruptions() -> _advance_flight_statuses() -> increment board.current_tick (lock-protected) -> advance sim_time by tick_duration -> if on_tick is not None, await on_tick(self.board). This last call is the sole integration point for the agent graph; simulator.py has no import of agents/graph.py at all.
  - run(num_ticks): calls run_tick() num_ticks times
  - run_until(stop_time): calls run_tick() repeatedly while sim_time < stop_time

=== data/seed_disruptions.py (ground truth) ===
generate_mock_disruptions(flights: list[Flight], seed: int | None = None) -> list[DisruptionEvent]
  - Takes flights as an explicit parameter, does not generate its own, so it works unchanged whether flights came from generate_mock_flights() or a real schedule.
  - Produces exactly 3 DisruptionEvents, sorted by trigger_time:
    1. DIS-WEATHER-001: WEATHER, LEVEL_4, trigger_time = earliest_arrival + 25min, affected_flights = up to 5 flights arriving within the first 20 minutes (the bottleneck cluster from seed_flights.py). This is the designated demo centerpiece.
    2. DIS-TECH-001: TECH_ISSUE, LEVEL_2, trigger_time = a randomly chosen later flight's scheduled_arrival + 5min, affected_flights = that one flight only.
    3. DIS-CREW-001: CREW_SHORTAGE, LEVEL_3, trigger_time = the schedule's midpoint flight's scheduled_arrival, affected_flights = 2 randomly sampled non-adjacent flights (excluding the tech-issue target).
save_disruptions_to_json(disruptions, filepath): same JSON-dump pattern as seed_flights.save_flights_to_json, documented as the contract a future real disruption feed should also satisfy.
__main__: imports generate_mock_flights from seed_flights.py, both called with seed=42, writes to disruptions_dataset.json next to the script (same __file__-based path pattern as seed_flights.py).

